254	<p>Of climb batch 29&rsquo;s changes, two are new rules: the change to change 40, under which a numeral converts where it stands, in place of the rule that a numeral has a type of its own; and change 70, under which an expression of numerals alone folds exactly, on both paths. Change 81 puts the last sentence of change 70, that a test of run-time arithmetic needs a variable operand, into the part on tests, in place of a workaround for a defect that the batch fixed. The other thirteen new items, and the changes to changes 39, 41, 42 and 68, state facts that the batch changed, name departures and traps that it found, or fix a word, an example or a citation.</p>
255	<p>Climb batch 29b landed on <code>main</code> at 23:37 UTC on 9 November, as the repair batch after climb batch 29. It built the curator&rsquo;s decisions on a numeral&rsquo;s type: a numeral&rsquo;s type exists only at compile time, with the numeral&rsquo;s four numbers as its static arguments, and a number type takes a numeral through a coercion generic over those numbers, which gives one value for each place where the numeral is written. It took away the numeral types&rsquo; getters, and the compiler&rsquo;s prelude now follows the same design. So the landing made sentences of the skill false again. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, at 03:30, 03:32 and 03:42 UTC on 10 November. The first installed the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: its judge said to install two of its sections, with two corrections. Its changes to the passages of changes 40, 41 and 70 are folded into those items, each with a reason headed &ldquo;Why, climb batch 29b&rdquo;, and changes 84 and 85 are its other changes. The second commit brought <code>SKILL.md</code>, <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>compiler.md</code> and <code>library.md</code> in line with the landing. Its changes to the passages of changes 42, 68, 75 and 78 are folded into those items, and change 86 is its other change. A cold read of both commits then found passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search, and the skill writer fixed most of them in the third commit, which also took the second commit&rsquo;s sentence in <code>library.md</code> back out. Each item shows the net change of the three commits.</p>
256	<p>Of climb batch 29b&rsquo;s changes, five change or add rules: the change to change 40, under which a numeral&rsquo;s type has its four numbers as static arguments and a program&rsquo;s own number type takes a numeral through a coercion generic over them, in place of a coercion that reads getters; the change to change 41, which gives the rule&rsquo;s answer for <code>=</code> between a number and a numeral, and the form to write; the change to change 70, under which a folded constant reaches a program&rsquo;s own type only through a coercion from its default type; the change to change 75, under which a numeral type&rsquo;s numbers, used as values, are <code>ZZ</code> values; and change 86, under which the prelude&rsquo;s numerals are the one exception to the rule that adds nothing to the prelude. The others, changes 84 and 85 and the changes to changes 42, 68 and 78, state facts that the batch changed, name departures and traps that it found, or cut words that state no rule.</p>
257	<p>Climb batch 30 landed on <code>main</code> on 13 November. It made the compiled path run a call&rsquo;s arguments as parallel tasks, as the language&rsquo;s rule asks, fixed defects of the compiled <code>atomic</code> block and <code>try</code>, and fixed four departures on numerals that changes 40, 42 and 68 named (rows 958, 967, 971 and 974). So the landing made sentences of the skill false again. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, two at 22:41 and one at 22:46 UTC on 13 November. The first installed the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: its judge said to install six of its seven sections, and held one, &ldquo;Static parameters, <code>where</code> clauses and sizes&rdquo;, which keeps its text. Beside the landing&rsquo;s one correction, on a numeral that rounds to an infinity, this update took in the findings of its own cold read, so it reworded sentences in each of the six sections. Its changes to the passages of changes 40, 41, 51, 70 and 85 are folded into those items, each with a reason headed &ldquo;Why, climb batch 30&rdquo;, and changes 87 to 100 are its other changes. The second commit brought <code>compiler.md</code>, <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>gate.md</code> and <code>tests-writing.md</code> in line with the landing. Its changes to the passages of changes 42, 68 and 78 are folded into those items, and changes 101 to 105 are its other changes. A cold read of both commits then found nineteen passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search. The skill writer fixed sixteen in the third commit and left three, which the items of changes 89, 96 and 98 name. Each item shows the net change of the three commits.</p>
258	<p>Of climb batch 30&rsquo;s changes, three are new rules: change 102, under which no compiled program relies on the order in which a call&rsquo;s arguments are evaluated; change 103, under which an <code>atomic</code> block or a <code>try</code> is bound in a statement of its own before it is passed as a later argument; and change 105, under which a test of parallelism under walk needs a range longer than 16. The change to change 40 adds the way to get an infinity. The other sixteen new items, and the changes to changes 41, 42, 51, 68, 70, 78 and 85, state facts that the batch changed, reword sentences that a reader could take two ways, or cut words that state no rule.</p>
259	<p>Climb batch 32 landed on <code>main</code> on 16 November. On both paths, it let a numeral run a more specific declaration than the one that fits its default type. It made the checker take the unwritten static argument of a reduction such as <code>SUM</code> from the reduction&rsquo;s expected type, or else from its body. It let a reference to a generic function take its instance from the arrow type that receives it. And it fixed walk&rsquo;s answer for <code>u = 0</code>, which changes 41 and 42 named (rows 973 and 966). So the landing made sentences of the skill false again. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, at 07:10, 07:13 and 07:19 UTC on 16 November. The first installed the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: its judge said to install the three sections that it rewrote, &ldquo;Static parameters, <code>where</code> clauses and sizes&rdquo;, &ldquo;Numbers&rdquo; and &ldquo;Reductions and comprehensions&rdquo;, and held none. Its changes to the passages of changes 41, 71 and 99 are folded into those items, each with a reason headed &ldquo;Why, climb batch 32&rdquo;, and changes 106 to 108 are its other changes. The second commit brought <code>SKILL.md</code>, <code>compiler.md</code>, <code>gate.md</code>, <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>library.md</code> and <code>tests-writing.md</code> in line with the landing. Its changes to the passages of changes 42, 68 and 76 are folded into those items, and changes 109 to 117 are its other changes. A cold read of both commits then found twenty-three passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search. The skill writer fixed twenty-two in the third commit and left one, which the item of change 71 names. Each item shows the net change of the three commits. Changes 76 and 99 have new titles, since their passages now say other things.</p>
260	<p>Of climb batch 32&rsquo;s changes, three change or add rules of the language as built: change 108, under which a numeral can run a more specific declaration than the one that fits its default type; the change to change 71, under which a tie between declarations that apply only by coercion goes to the one that takes the call&rsquo;s numerals at their default; and the change to change 99, under which the checker takes a reduction&rsquo;s unwritten static argument from the expected type or the body, and a worker writes the argument at four places only. The change to change 76 states the rule of change 108 in <code>SKILL.md</code>. Change 116 changes an instruction: the element type of <code>SUM</code> and <code>PROD</code> is written in the library, and no longer everywhere. Change 117 is a new rule for the Fortress of a test: parenthesise a call or a subscript before a superscript. The other nine new items, and the changes to changes 41, 42 and 68, state facts that the batch changed, name departures and traps that it found, each with the way round it, or reword a sentence that a reader could take two ways.</p>
